\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Discrete induction and its open interfaces}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{How can algebra and geometry organize induction and background
variation? [Gaussian elimination](fdip-000F) gives a finite example;
the [synthesis conjecture](fdip-003X) and
[finite interfaces](fdip-003Y) distinguish this result from the
further maps required.}

\p{The route through these explorations is the diagram opening the
[compatibility test](fdip-009Y). That test fixes one finite spinor
datum. The [larger proposals](fdip-00A2) remain open tests.
The conjecture and finite interfaces below remain distinct from
those open questions.}

\p{Finite-group symmetry separates Gaussian fields into sectors.
On a periodic grid, each momentum orbit carries a representation
induced from one block (\ref{fdip-016E}), and signed coordinate changes
have spin lifts on a graded carrier (\ref{fdip-016U}). Selected
backgrounds and connections lead to finite gauge fields. The Euclidean
time results include reflection positivity and
the [time map on spinor fields](fdip-017Z), followed by explicit
transfer operators and Gauss's law.
Grassmann integration and fermionic elimination precede the covariant
Dirac operator, the free Wilson remedy, the graded kernel index,
signed zeros on the interpolated momentum grid, Ginsparg–Wilson operators,
staggered reductions, the index in a gauge field, charged rotations of
several species and sampled symbols. For [graded spin fields](fdip-0170) one can transport cyclic degrees,
test for charge conjugation and exchange of sectors, and compare
determinants under total inversion.
For scalar fields on group orbits, word traces (\ref{fdip-018Y}) test the
conjugation symmetry of a pair of coupling products. Reduced links
also give the exact one-edge transfer. The arrow from gauge
fields to Euclidean time, drawn in the [reader guide](fdip-004E),
is a reading prerequisite for the [gauge transfer](fdip-007Y) and
for [Gauss's law](fdip-0088). Reflection positivity alone does not
supply that time shift. The arrow from gauge fields to lattice
fermions is a reading prerequisite for the covariant Dirac
operator and for the free Wilson remedy that follows it, not for
Grassmann integration or fermionic elimination, and not an
identification of those operators with a physical electron.}

\p{The comparisons that remain open are these. The
[physical environment map](fdip-003X) #{E} is not constructed.
The [supplied-circle limit](fdip-004J) controls one fixed Fourier
mode; it is not a continuum limit of the full induced law, and no
moduli map controls that limit. The
[enriched background groupoid](fdip-000X) and the
[geometric realization](fdip-004L) do not supply a site, a stack,
or a descent that organizes those families.
The [larger proposals](fdip-00A2) remain open tests.
[What the structure fixes and what it leaves free](fdip-009G)
records that comparison. The chapters transcluded by
[Structure](fdip-008R) develop the questions of that part.
None of these absences is a no-go theorem or a physical
identification.}

\p{\strong{Wilson's claims.} The tested claims are the two finite
maps of \ref{fdip-00A0}: conjugation #{SU(2)\to SO(3)} and the
quotient #{2T\to A_4}, both with kernel #{\{1,-1\}}. The larger
proposals in \ref{fdip-00A2} stay open. Three of the 4 Lie
names do not occur in the cited Wilson paper, and the Hessian
and #{SL(4,\mathbb R)} claims are recorded there as
interpretations. The absence of a construction is not a no-go
theorem and not a physical identification.}

\p{\strong{Next questions.} What remains can be stated as 4
questions. The [physical environment map](fdip-003X) #{E} is
still unconstructed. The supplied-circle comparison of one
Fourier mode remains \ref{fdip-004J}. The circle family is stated
in \ref{fdip-00E1}, and the hierarchical family is stated in
\ref{fdip-00E2}. For the gasket graphs, \ref{fdip-00E3} records
finite trace identities, \ref{fdip-00EN} gives a compact resistance
completion, and \ref{fdip-00F5} gives the counting exponent. These
statements are inside [[fdip-00AV]].
The higher-dimensional
area law, fermion reflection positivity and fermion transfer,
and infinite-volume reconstruction were not proved. The [enriched background groupoid](fdip-000X)
and the [geometric realization](fdip-004L) still do not supply a
site, a stack, or a descent. Whether the context square organizes
the selectors remains open, and no stack is proved.
Wilson's finite maps are tested, and the larger proposals remain
open tests.}

